WEBVTT
00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:11.440
<v Ira Glass>All right, now this moment from the life of a teenage girl in these United States.
<v Ira Glass>好了，现在来看看美国少女生活中的这一刻。

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:18.550
<v Ira Glass>School has just let out. Perfect spring day. Kara and her boyfriend, Jeremy, come outside, and he jumps on her back.
<v Ira Glass>学校刚放学，正是完美的春日。卡拉和她的男朋友杰里米走到户外，他跳到她的背上。

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.400
<v Kara>Get off me. Jeremy, come on. You're hurting my back.
<v Kara>从我身上下来。杰里米，得了吧。你弄疼我的背了。

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.100
<v Jeremy>You love it.
<v Jeremy>你很享受。

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:23.770
<v Kara>I don't.
<v Kara>我才没有。

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:33.300
<v Ira Glass>When I recorded this, I'd been doing documentary radio stories on Kara and her friends for two years. So it was natural that at this point, Kara would just turn to me and say--
<v Ira Glass>我录制这段的时候，我已经跟拍卡拉和她的朋友们做纪录片电台节目两年了。所以此时此刻，卡拉自然而然地转向我说——

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:42.670
<v Kara>Ira, kick his butt. Get away from me. You're too violent. Jeremy, stop.
<v Kara>艾拉，教训他一下。离我远点。你太粗鲁了。杰里米，住手。

00:00:42.670 --> 00:01:12.870
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, the lives of teenage girls. From WBEZ Chicago, you're listening to This American Life. I'm Ira Glass, back for another week, to kick your butt. Not really. Back for another week documenting everyday stories of these United States using all the tools of radio storytelling-- documentaries, monologues, overheard conversations, found tapes, anything we can think of.
<v Ira Glass>好了，今天我们的节目主题是少女的生活。这里是WBEZ芝加哥电台，你正在收听的是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯，又回来陪大家一周，顺便教训一下你们。开个玩笑。我又回来做一周关于美国日常故事的记录，运用各种广播叙事手法——纪录片、独白、偶然听到的对话、现成的磁带，任何我们能想到的手段。

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:42.180
<v Ira Glass>And to consider the question of what it's like to be a teenager in America today, consider this incident between Kara and Jeremy and their friends. They had just come outside after a rehearsal for a school play that Kara is putting together based on the writings of Henry Rollins. And if you don't know his work, Henry Rollins is a pop star. He plays this sort of testosterone-loaded post-punk rock. And at the time of this recording, he was Kara's favorite writer.
<v Ira Glass>为了探讨“当今美国的少女生活是什么样”这个问题，不妨看看卡拉、杰里米和他们的朋友们之间发生的这件小事。他们刚从学校话剧排练现场出来，卡拉正在编排这部剧，剧本基于亨利·罗林斯的文章。如果你不了解他的作品，亨利·罗林斯是一位流行歌星。他玩的是那种荷尔蒙爆棚的后朋克摇滚。在那次录音时，他是卡拉最喜欢的作家。

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.870
<v Ira Glass>So, Kara, do you have a favorite piece in the Rollins show?
<v Ira Glass>那么，卡拉，你在罗林斯的演出中最喜欢哪一段？

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.210
<v Kara>"Ladies, you want equality."
<v Kara>“女士们，你们想要平等。”

00:01:47.210 --> 00:02:13.840
<v Ira Glass>She pulls out the papers that comprise the show's script, and she reads to me. And I should say here that Rollins and our 18-year-old teenager get rather graphic here in a way that might not be suitable for younger listeners. Rollins, in this poem, says that guys are basically scum. Basically just scum. His vision is very unambiguous. They're scum. And he gives heartfelt advice to all women who he addresses as "ladies."
<v Ira Glass>她拿出构成演出剧本的纸张，读给我听。我得说，罗林斯和我们这位18岁的少女在这里谈论的内容相当露骨，可能不太适合年纪较小的听众。罗林斯在这首诗中说，男人基本上就是人渣。简直就是人渣。他的观点非常明确。他们就是人渣。他给所有女性——他称之为“女士们”——提出了衷心的建议。

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:30.090
<v Kara>"If you want equality to men, it's not going to come to you in the mail or in your sleep. You're going to have to take it, grab hold of it, wrench it out of their hairy-fisted grasp. What I'm getting to is this-- go to the gunsmiths, get a 0.357 magnum with a long barrel. Load it. Put in your bag. Go on with your life.
<v Kara>“如果你想要与男性平等的权利，它不会通过邮件寄给你，也不会在你睡梦中到来。你必须去争取，紧紧抓住它，从他们那双长满老茧的手中夺过来。我要说的是——去枪械店，买一把长枪管的0.357麦格农手枪。装上子弹。放进包里。继续过你的生活。

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:53.320
<v Kara>OK, it's just another day. You're walking down the street. You pass this gas station. Some gas station attendant is standing there, glaring at you, checking you out, smiling at you. This ugly, greasy, stupid guy comes over to you and blocks your way as you try to walk down the street. He breathes on you and says, hey, baby, looky here, seven inches. You pull out the gun, stick the barrel in his face, and scream, I've got eight mother [BLEEP], I've got eight.
<v Kara>好了，这只是平常的一天。你走在街上。经过一个加油站。有个加油站员工站在那儿，盯着你看，打量你，对你笑。这个又丑又油腻的蠢货走过来，在你试图过马路时挡住你的去路。他对着你呼气，说，嘿，宝贝，看看这儿，有七英寸。你掏出枪，把枪管顶在他脸上，尖叫道，老娘有八英寸，你这（哔）养的，我有八英寸。

00:02:53.320 --> 00:03:03.460
<v Kara>There. Now you're speaking in terms he can must surely identify with. You're not only equal. You dominate. Believe me, it's come to this. Take advantage of their weakness. They take advantage of yours. You're entitled."
<v Kara>瞧，现在你用的语言他绝对能听懂。你不仅平等了，而且你占了上风。相信我，事情已经到了这一步。利用他们的软弱。他们也在利用你的。这是你应得的。”

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:14.320
<v Ira Glass>The guys in the group all look at each other. Finally, Kara's boyfriend, Jeremy, says, "There's nothing wrong with a guy looking at you and thinking how good you look." Kara tells him that isn't the point.
<v Ira Glass>小组里的男生们面面相觑。最后，卡拉的男朋友杰里米说：“男生看你一眼，觉得你好看，这没什么错。”卡拉告诉他，重点不在这里。

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:23.510
<v Kara>No. They say things. They'll follow you. They'll come up to you, and they'll talk to you like this. And they'll make sure that they do that, that their pelvis is pushing against you here.
<v Kara>不。他们会说些话。他们会跟踪你。他们会靠近你，然后像这样跟你搭话。而且他们会确保这样做，确保他们的骨盆像这样贴着你。

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.080
<v Jeremy>I have never, ever seen anybody do that, though.
<v Jeremy>但我从来没见过有人那么做。

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.870
<v Kara>But you're not a woman, though.
<v Kara>但你又不是女人。

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.060
<v Ira Glass>Kara tells him a story about some guy who was staring at her in a pool hall.
<v Ira Glass>卡拉给他讲了一个故事，说有个男的在台球厅一直盯着她看。

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:44.320
<v Kara>So when I was leaving, he went-- he came up real close to me. He's like, "Bye." I turned around, and I was like, "I hate rude men!" I screamed it, and then the entire pool hall was like-- and I was like--
<v Kara>我离开的时候，他走过来——他靠得很近。他说：“再见。”我转过身，我说：“我讨厌无礼的男人！”我大叫起来，然后整个台球厅都……我就像……

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:51.280
<v Jeremy>Man. You guys are rough. I'm never saying bye to a girl again in my life.
<v Jeremy>天呐。你们太粗暴了。我这辈子再也不跟女生说再见了。

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:58.580
<v Ira Glass>Mary's boyfriend, Aaron, has joined the conversation. "So how should we treat girls?" He asks. "If we like a girl, how do we let her know without offending her?"
<v Ira Glass>玛丽的男朋友亚伦加入了谈话。“那么我们该如何对待女生呢？”他问。“如果我们喜欢一个女生，该怎么让她知道而不冒犯她？”

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:04.460
<v Aaron>Right. That's why I'm asking how should we do it. And sometimes we don't know. And sometimes we don't know.
<v Aaron>对啊。所以我才问我们该怎么做。有时候我们不知道。有时候真的不知道。

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:06.420
<v Mary>Don't look at women like they're pieces of meat. Because they're not.
<v Mary>不要把女性看作是某种肉块。因为她们不是。

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:18.850
<v Aaron>But what if they're dressed like that and want to be looked at like that? There's always that instinct in the guy. And if he sees something he really likes, he's going to look at it. If you would see a guy that you really like and really think is hot, you don't sit here--
<v Aaron>但如果她们穿成那样，想被那样看呢？男人总是有这种本能。如果他看到自己真正喜欢的东西，他就会去看。如果你看到一个你非常喜欢、觉得很火辣的男生，你不会坐在这儿——

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:20.010
<v Aaron>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Aaron>（众声喧哗）

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:31.100
<v Aaron>Wait, wait, wait. Listen to me. Listen to me. Listen to me. One, two, microphone check. Wait, listen. You're going to say, if a guy walks by that has a real nice butt that you like, you're not going to be like, damn.
<v Aaron>等等，等等，等等。听我说。听我说。听我说。一、二，麦克风测试。等等，听着。你会说，如果有个屁股很翘的男生路过，是你喜欢的类型，你难道不会心想，哇塞。

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:54.550
<v Ira Glass>They talk about this for 20 minutes. The boys ask over and over, what should we do? What do you want us to do? How should we act? And over and over, the girls name a few simple rules that anyone could follow if they pay attention. One, do not invade a girl's personal space. Two, don't say anything rude. Aaron says that certain things are only seen as rude if the girl doesn't like you. If she thinks you're cute, then they're OK.
<v Ira Glass>他们谈论这个话题足足20分钟。男生们一遍又一遍地问，我们该怎么做？你希望我们怎么做？我们该表现得如何？女生们一遍又一遍地说出一些简单的规则，只要稍加注意，任何人都能做到。第一，不要侵犯女生的私人空间。第二，不要说无礼的话。亚伦说，有些事情只有在女生不喜欢你的时候才会被视为无礼。如果她觉得你很帅，那这些行为就没问题。

00:04:54.550 --> 00:05:00.030
<v Aaron>Say if I was real-- if I was good looking, and I was sitting there staring at you, and if I was all--
<v Aaron>比如如果我很……如果我长得帅，我坐在那里盯着你看，如果我一直……

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:01.550
<v Mary>But I don't want to talk to a dork.
<v Mary>但我不想跟傻瓜说话。

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:08.930
<v Aaron>But how does he know that? If I was a dork, maybe I think I'm looking. What if we're just at the staring stage?
<v Aaron>但他怎么知道呢？如果我是个傻瓜，也许我以为我在看。如果我们只是处于盯着看的阶段呢？

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.300
<v Mary>No, no. Because if you don't know somebody, you've got to go by their--
<v Mary>不，不。因为如果你不认识某人，你必须通过他们的……

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:36.900
<v Ira Glass>What's striking is that there are certain moments when it actually seems like the boys really do want to know how they should act. They really want to know. And in those moments it's clear that this conversation could only happen in high school. After high school, everyone's opinion about these questions is pretty much set. But in high school, sometimes there's still a feeling that everything's up for grabs. So you can tell your boyfriend not to be a jerk, and he might actually listen.
<v Ira Glass>引人注目的是，在某些时刻，男生们似乎真的想知道他们该如何表现。他们真的很想知道。在这些时刻，很明显，这种对话只能发生在高中。高中之后，每个人对这些问题的看法基本定型了。但在高中，有时仍有一种感觉，一切皆有可能。所以你可以告诉你的男朋友不要做个混蛋，而他真的可能会听进去。

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:49.490
<v Ira Glass>That's a good side of everything being up for grabs. There's also a bad side. With everything up for grabs, it's hard to know how you're supposed to act. Which brings us to today's program.
<v Ira Glass>一切皆有可能的一面是好的。也有坏的一面。当一切皆有可能时，很难知道自己该如何行事。这就引出了今天的节目。

00:05:49.490 --> 00:06:03.820
<v Ira Glass>Our program today is made up of three stories about teenage girls who have to decide how they're going to act in situations where the ground rules are unclear and very malleable. Act One of our program, Jo Carol Pierce bends the rules in a small town in Texas.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目由三个关于少女的故事组成，她们必须决定在基本规则不明确且非常多变的情况下，自己该如何行事。节目的第一幕，乔·卡罗尔·皮尔斯在德克萨斯州的一个小镇上打破了规则。

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:09.390
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Chicago writer Rennie Sparks struggles without rules in suburban America, somewhere.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，芝加哥作家蕾妮·斯帕克斯在美国某个郊区，在没有规则的情况下挣扎。

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:19.100
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, a man tries to lay down rules for a girl. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，一个男人试图为一个女孩定下规矩。请继续收听。

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:23.030
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Texas Girl.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，德克萨斯女孩。

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:42.500
<v Ira Glass>Jo Carol Pierce is this performer from Austin, Texas, who just put out her first album at the age of 51, squarely outside of the teenager category. But she talks about her teenage years in the record. And she's working this really unusual style on this record. The CD is called Bad Girls Upset By the Truth. I have it here.
<v Ira Glass>乔·卡罗尔·皮尔斯是来自德克萨斯州奥斯汀的一位表演者，她在51岁时才发行了自己的第一张专辑，显然已经脱离了青少年的范畴。但她在唱片中谈到了自己的少女时代。她在唱片中运用了一种非常独特的风格。这张CD叫《被真相困扰的坏女孩》。我这里就有。

00:06:42.500 --> 00:07:13.530
<v Ira Glass>And over the course of the record, she tells this funny, emotional story punctuated periodically by songs she's written. It's a version of American musical theater, one of the most American cultural forms. It's a kind of American musical theater that no one is bothering with, except for her. And it's the kind of CD that is not going to get much radio air play anywhere, and so we thought that we would begin today's show by playing you just a chunk of this record, a couple songs and some of the story.
<v Ira Glass>在整张唱片中，她讲述了一个有趣、感人的故事，中间不时穿插着她创作的歌曲。这是一种美国音乐剧，是美国文化中最具代表性的形式之一。这是一种除了她之外没人愿意尝试的美国音乐剧。这张CD很难在任何地方获得电台播放，所以我们想在今天的节目开头，为大家播放其中的一部分，包括几首歌和一些故事片段。

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:31.670
<v Ira Glass>And the story that she tells on the record begins with a suicide attempt, a teenager's suicide attempt. In real life, Jo Carol says that she thought about suicide well into her 30s, and she also worked at a suicide hotline. And the suicide on the record comes during a fight with her boyfriend.
<v Ira Glass>她在唱片中讲述的故事始于一次自杀未遂，一个少女的自杀未遂。在现实生活中，乔·卡罗尔说她直到30多岁还想过自杀，她还曾在自杀干预热线工作过。而在唱片里，自杀发生在一次与男友的争吵中。

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:55.260
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Me and my boyfriend were driving around the loop, and we were having a terrible fight. I remember that he called me a son of a bitch. And that confused me, so I opened the passenger door and flung myself into traffic. First, I was hit by a pink Coupe de Ville, knocked me into the path of an out of state semi and just squished me flat into that burning asphalt.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>我和男友开着车在环路上兜风，我们正在进行一场可怕的争吵。我记得他骂我是个婊子养的。这让我很困惑，所以我打开副驾驶车门，把自己甩进了车流中。首先，我被一辆粉色的凯迪拉克撞了，把我撞向一辆外州的大货车路径上，直接把我压扁在那燃烧的沥青路上。

00:07:55.260 --> 00:08:23.980
<v Jo Carol Pierce>But after I had committed suicide, I started seeing things in a whole new light. I noticed what a cute boy my boyfriend was. Couldn't understand why I'd been calling him a decorated rat only moments before my fatal plunge. I took his hand. It was chaffed and slightly smaller than mine. We went home, and I made him some biscuits and gravy from scratch, because I really wanted to.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>但在我自杀之后，我开始以全新的视角看世界。我注意到我的男朋友是多么可爱的男孩。我不明白为什么在我致命的一跳前几分钟，我竟然一直叫他装点门面的老鼠。我牵起他的手。他的手有些磨损，比我的稍微小一点。我们回家了，我亲手为他做了饼干和肉汁，因为我真心想这么做。

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:47.830
<v Jo Carol Pierce>It's good to commit suicide when there's something you just can't figure out. Like when I hit high school and I found out you're a bitch if you don't and a whore if you do. Commit suicide when you've got a burning question that no one can answer for you. Mine was, what are these boys for, and what am I supposed to do with them? Don't get upset about the war in the Middle East. Do something. Kill yourself.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>当你遇到无法理解的事情时，自杀是个好主意。比如当我上高中时，我发现如果你不那么做，你就是个婊子，如果你做了，你就是个荡妇。当你有一个没人能回答的尖锐问题时，就去自杀吧。我的问题是，这些男生是为了什么，我该拿他们怎么办？不要为中东的战争感到难过。做点什么吧。去自杀吧。

00:08:47.830 --> 00:09:12.680
<v Jo Carol Pierce>It's good to commit suicide over small things that other people might neglect, like grocery shopping. I can't imagine walking into that ATB without first committing suicide. Think about it. Commit suicide first thing in the morning. And that way, you feel better all day. Do it your first day on a new job, and the next day you can call in dead. Do it early in a love affair and just get it over with.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>为了那些其他人可能会忽视的小事而自杀是个好选择，比如去杂货店购物。我无法想象在不先自杀的情况下走进那家ATB超市。想一想。每天早上第一件事就是自杀。这样，你一整天都会感觉好一点。第一天上班时做一次，第二天你就可以打电话说你死了。在恋情开始时做一次，直接了当。

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:25.050
<v Jo Carol Pierce>That's why me and the boys are going to commit suicide right now. We hope that you will join us. Just start getting really pitiful and morose, and in the third verse, we will slit our wrists.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>这就是为什么我和男生们现在要一起自杀。希望你们能加入我们。开始变得可怜巴巴、闷闷不乐，在第三段歌词时，我们会割开手腕。

00:09:25.050 --> 00:12:59.550
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[MUSIC - BY JO CAROL PIERCE AND BAND]
<v Jo Carol Pierce>（音乐 - 乔·卡罗尔·皮尔斯及乐队）

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:17.520
<v Ira Glass>The main story of Bad Girls Upset By the Truth is this man, or this series of men, actually, who appear in Jo Carol's life. She calls them Secret Dans. And she falls for them, sleeps with them, and it's a lot of them. More, in fact, than anyone around her thinks is proper.
<v Ira Glass>《被真相困扰的坏女孩》的主线故事是关于一个男人，或者说是一系列男人，他们出现在乔·卡罗尔的生活中。她称他们为“秘密丹”。她爱上了他们，与他们上床，而且人数很多。事实上，比她周围任何人认为的都要多。

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:40.300
<v Jo Carol Pierce>I got in a world of trouble going from Secret Dan to Secret Dan. I got a bad reputation in my peer group. Lots of my friends were getting married, and when they did, they would just drop me flat. And my friends that weren't real mad at me were real worried about me. And everybody was discouraging me from falling in love. They said, Jo Carol, why don't you do something you're good at?
<v Jo Carol Pierce>我在从一个“秘密丹”转向另一个“秘密丹”的过程中遇到了无穷无尽的麻烦。我在同龄人中名声扫地。我的很多朋友都结婚了，当她们结婚时，就会直接抛弃我。而那些没对我非常生气的也是真心为我担忧。每个人都在劝我不要坠入爱河。她们说，乔·卡罗尔，为什么不做点你擅长的事呢？

00:13:40.300 --> 00:14:22.770
<v Jo Carol Pierce>So I tried to explain to them why I had to do these very upsetting things. I said, look, the reason that I cannot pass up a single Secret Dan is that each one of them is just another side of Jesus. And I know that because every time I kiss another one, I can feel Jesus right through his skin. And I need to know Jesus fully. Jesus in a brown leather jacket. Or the kind of thuggy Jesus with the hooded eyes like Robert Mitchum. Or the Jesus you want to make biscuits for. Or the Jesus you want to wrestle with. Or the Catholic Jesus. Or the Jesus who's so good in bed you think he's Catholic, but really he's not. There was even a Yankee Jesus.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>所以我试着向她们解释我为什么必须做这些令人沮丧的事情。我说，看，我无法拒绝任何一个“秘密丹”的原因是，他们每一个人都只是耶稣的另一面。我知道这一点，因为每次我亲吻另一个人时，我都能透过他的皮肤感受到耶稣。我需要完全了解耶稣。穿着棕色皮夹克的耶稣。或者那种带着像罗伯特·米彻姆那样兜帽眼睛的强盗耶稣。或者是你想为他做饼干的耶稣。或者是你想和他摔跤的耶稣。或者是天主教徒的耶稣。或者是床上功夫好到让你以为他是天主教徒，但实际上他不是的耶稣。甚至还有一个洋基耶稣。

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:51.130
<v Jo Carol Pierce>And my friends said, nuh-uh. But I could tell my friends were upset by the truth, because they'd go off in small groups and whisper. And every once in a while I would hear this phrase, "mental hospital." And it made me nervous, so I got married. And when my husband slipped that golden wedding ring on my finger, it was the first time I'd ever strayed off of that spiritual path that Jesus had set out for me so specifically years before.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>朋友们说，不行。但我看得出来，朋友们被真相困扰了，因为她们会聚成小群窃窃私语。偶尔我会听到“精神病院”这个词。这让我很紧张，所以我结婚了。当我的丈夫把那枚金戒指套在我的手指上时，那是我第一次偏离了耶稣多年前为我设定的那个精神道路。

00:14:51.130 --> 00:15:18.470
<v Jo Carol Pierce>It was back in high school. One sunny night I was out jumping bar ditches in my parents' car when it overheated and stalled and left me stranded out there on the Idalou highway. I didn't know what I was going to do until this darling boy named Joey came along in his shiny truck, and he fixed my car on the spot. And then he witnessed to me about Jesus. It was his Highway Witness for Jesus Program.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>那是在高中。一个阳光明媚的夜晚，我开着父母的车在田间小路上颠簸，车子过热熄火，把我困在了伊达卢高速公路上。我不知道该怎么办，直到这个叫乔伊的可爱男孩开着闪亮的卡车经过，他当场修好了我的车。然后，他向我传达了耶稣的福音。那是他的“公路见证耶稣计划”。

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:49.080
<v Jo Carol Pierce>And he was so darling that I just went to church with him that night. I liked sitting next to him so much, and before I knew what I was doing, I had grabbed his hand. He jerked and turned pale, but he did not get up and move. His heart was beating just like a hammer in the palm of his hand, and it was pounding home the sermon about the burning bush and the tongues of flame like never before. And I knew I had to keep that beat forever and ever.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>他太可爱了，那天晚上我就跟他去了教堂。我非常喜欢坐在他旁边，还没等我反应过来，我就抓住了他的手。他猛地一缩，脸色苍白，但他没有站起来离开。他的心脏在他手心里像锤子一样跳动，那跳动声仿佛在重申那篇关于燃烧的灌木和火焰的舌头的布道，从未如此强烈。我知道我必须永远守住那份节奏。

00:15:49.080 --> 00:16:29.320
<v Jo Carol Pierce>And then we watched the preacher baptize Naomi [? Stutler. ?] And maybe it was the way her back arched when he pulled her up out of the water, or maybe it was the way that white robe clung to her body that inspired me to the point that I knew Jesus in my heart for the very first time, because he said something right in my ear. He said, "Don't you ever even worry about keeping that beat. You just let that beat keep you." I pretty much knew what was going to happen with the boys if I let that beat keep me, and I wasn't all that surprised that Jesus wanted me to go all the way. Because, after all, he did.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>然后我们看着传教士为娜奥米受洗。也许是她从水中被拉出来时背部弯曲的样子，或者是那件白袍贴在她身上的样子，激励了我，让我第一次在心里感受到了耶稣，因为他就在我耳边说了一些话。他说：“别担心守住那节奏。你只要让那节奏守住你就好。”我基本知道如果让那节奏守住我，我和男生之间会发生什么，对于耶稣想要我彻底奉献，我并不感到惊讶。因为毕竟，他也曾那样做过。

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:49.950
<v Jo Carol Pierce>I took my first step on my new spiritual path that evening after church when I suggested to Joey that we park at a prairie dog camp and discuss Jesus a little further. Later on, Joey was real disappointed in himself for what happened out there, but I wasn't. My new spiritual path was just like walking on cloud nine.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>那天晚上教堂礼拜后，我迈出了新精神道路的第一步，当时我建议乔伊把车停在草原犬营地，更深入地讨论耶稣。后来，乔伊对自己在那儿发生的事情感到非常失望，但我没有。我的新精神道路就像走在云端一样。

00:16:49.950 --> 00:17:15.340
<v Jo Carol Pierce>I didn't hear from Joey for quite a while, but I didn't think anything about it. Because I had asked my question, what are these boys for, and what am I supposed to do with them in the plural. And it had been answered in the plural. And so I was real busy. But one evening Joey threw pebbles at my window, and I sneaked out behind the playhouse to meet him. And I found out why I had not heard from him was he had been saving up to buy me something very special.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>我有很长一段时间没再见到乔伊，但我没多想。因为我提出了我的问题，这些男生是为了什么，而我该拿他们这些复数的人怎么办？答案已经以复数形式给出了。所以我很忙。但有一天晚上，乔伊往我窗户扔石子，我偷偷溜到凉亭后面去见他。我发现之所以一直没收到他的消息，是因为他一直在存钱想给我买件非常特别的礼物。

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:18.510
<v Jo Carol Pierce>But unfortunately, the day he got it out of layaway was the very--
<v Jo Carol Pierce>但不幸的是，他从代购店取回礼物的日子正好是……

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:23.020
<v Joey>Jo Carol, I heard you drove through the [? Hidey-Ho ?] take-out window naked today after school.
<v Joey>乔·卡罗尔，我听说你今天放学后裸体开车经过了“海迪霍”外卖窗口。

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:53.990
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Oh god, I am so relieved. I was afraid I had made that whole thing up in my head and was turning out to be crazy like Mama. Because, see, what happened was I drove up, Buddy [? Grays ?] gives me my change and my chili dog. And I just sit there waiting for something to happen. And finally I say, "Buddy." He says, "What?" He says, "Hi." He says, "Hi." And I drove off feeling like I wasn't even in this world. I am so relieved somebody besides me thinks that happened.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>哦天呐，我真是太如释重负了。我一直担心那整件事是我脑子里编造出来的，担心我会变得像妈妈一样疯狂。你看，事情是这样的，我开车过去，巴迪给了我找零和热狗。我坐在那儿等着事情发生。最后我说，“巴迪。”他说，“什么？”他说，“嗨。”他说，“嗨。”我就开车走了，感觉自己好像根本不在这个世界上。有人除了我之外也觉得那件事发生了，我真是太如释重负了。

00:17:53.990 --> 00:17:56.520
<v Joey>How could you do this to me, Jo Carol?
<v Joey>你怎么能这样对我，乔·卡罗尔？

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:58.630
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Well, sometimes I get real bored.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>好吧，有时候我真的很无聊。

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:07.500
<v Joey>Well, if you're bored, there's plenty of constructive things you could do. You could raise a sheep or some chickens for the FFA. Or join the UFO Watch or go with me on the Highway Witness Program.
<v Joey>好吧，如果你无聊，有很多有建设性的事情可以做。你可以养只羊或者几只鸡参加FFA。或者加入UFO观察队，或者和我一起参加“公路见证计划”。

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:10.730
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Now, wait, Joey. What did I ever do to you?
<v Jo Carol Pierce>等等，乔伊。我到底对你做了什么？

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:12.600
<v Joey>You went out with Gary [? Cooney ?] tonight.
<v Joey>你今晚跟加里·库尼出去了。

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:13.760
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Yeah?
<v Jo Carol Pierce>是吗？

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:15.840
<v Joey>After he took Martha Lynn [? Searsey ?] home?
<v Joey>在他送玛莎·琳·瑟西回家之后？

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:17.020
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Yeah?
<v Jo Carol Pierce>是吗？

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:20.310
<v Joey>You ruined my life in my senior year.
<v Joey>你在我的高三毁了我的生活。

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:26.990
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Oh, Joey, I am just sick I ruined your senior year. But how did I do it exactly?
<v Jo Carol Pierce>哦，乔伊，我很难过我毁了你的高三。但我到底是怎样毁了它的？

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:30.190
<v Joey>I worked all my spare time to buy you this ring.
<v Joey>我所有的业余时间都在打工，就为了给你买这枚戒指。

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:35.740
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Oh, Joey, that is so pretty. Bless your heart, it really is.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>哦，乔伊，这真漂亮。保佑你的心，它真的很美。

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:38.070
<v Joey>It was my college education.
<v Joey>这是我的大学学费。

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:44.870
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Look how it sparkles almost like a genuine diamond. If I wore this, people might think it was really real.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>你看它闪闪发光，几乎就像一颗真正的钻石。如果我戴上它，人们可能会以为它是真的。

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:56.890
<v Ira Glass>And that's when he took it from me. And he pulled it across the glass of the playhouse window, leaving a very deep scratch there.
<v Ira Glass>就在那时，他从我手中夺过了戒指。他把它划过凉亭的玻璃窗，留下了一道很深的划痕。

00:18:56.890 --> 00:19:01.180
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[SINGING] This diamond is really real, Joey.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>（演唱）这颗钻石是真的，乔伊。

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:05.460
<v Joey>[SINGING] Do you think I'd buy you a rhinestone?
<v Joey>（演唱）你以为我会给你买个人造水钻吗？

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:07.740
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[SINGING] Do you really wanna marry me?
<v Jo Carol Pierce>（演唱）你真的想娶我吗？

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:12.120
<v Joey>No. [SINGING] I just wanna go on home.
<v Joey>不。（演唱）我只是想回家。

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:17.500
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[SINGING] Oh, Joey, how did it come to this? I'm so depressed.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>（演唱）哦，乔伊，怎么会变成这样？我太沮丧了。

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:23.990
<v Joey>Well, Jo Carol, why do you think they even call it premarital sex? What are you going to call it if you don't get married afterwards?
<v Joey>那么，乔·卡罗尔，你觉得他们为什么叫它婚前性行为呢？如果你婚后没有在一起，你打算管它叫什么？

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:26.900
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Oh, you could call it a whole lot of things. Like if you're trying to be--
<v Jo Carol Pierce>哦，你可以叫它很多名字。比如如果你想——

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:30.050
<v Joey>Oh, I don't want to hear.
<v Joey>哦，我不想听。

00:19:30.050 --> 00:20:02.710
<v Joey>[SINGING] I should have gone the way I came, like a flicker in her flame. Left her there remembering how my diamond scratched her window pane. Should have gone the way I came, in the darkness, in the rain. In the morning all that would remain is my scratch upon her window pane.
<v Joey>[歌唱] 我本该原路返回，就像她火焰中的一缕闪光。把她留在那里，回忆着我的钻石是如何划伤她的窗玻璃。我本该原路返回，在那黑暗中，在那雨中。清晨留下的只有我的划痕在她的窗玻璃上。

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:26.900
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[SINGING] I'm so flattered, I can't tell you. But Jesus has another plan for me. And, Joey, it'll be better for you, too. Some day you're gonna thank me for all the girls you would have missed and never kissed if I said yes.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[歌唱] 我受宠若惊，无法言表。但上帝对我另有安排。而且，乔伊，这对你也好。总有一天，你会感谢我，因为如果我答应了，你就会错过那些你本该遇到却从未亲吻过的女孩。

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:32.180
<v Joey>Jo Carol, you're only worried about all the boys you want to kiss and mess around with.
<v Joey>乔·卡罗尔，你只担心那些你想亲吻和鬼混的男孩。

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:34.910
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Well, that too, yes.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>嗯，那也是，没错。

00:20:34.910 --> 00:21:10.150
<v Joey>[SINGING] I should have gone the way I came, like a flicker in her flame, and left her there remembering how my diamond scratched her window pane. Should have gone the way I came, in the darkness in the rain, in the morning all that would remain is my scratch upon her window pane.
<v Joey>[歌唱] 我本该原路返回，就像她火焰中的一缕闪光，把她留在那里，回忆着我的钻石是如何划伤她的窗玻璃。我本该原路返回，在那黑暗中，在那雨中，清晨留下的只有我的划痕在她的窗玻璃上。

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:13.370
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[SINGING] So it really has to be goodbye?
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[歌唱] 所以真的非要说再见吗？

00:21:13.370 --> 00:21:17.920
<v Joey>[SINGING] I don't know what else I could do.
<v Joey>[歌唱] 我不知道还能做什么。

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:21.060
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[SINGING] One more time to remember me by?
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[歌唱] 再来一次，好让你记住我？

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:24.840
<v Joey>Don't worry. [SINGING] I'll remember you.
<v Joey>别担心。[歌唱] 我会记住你的。

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:28.710
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[SINGING] One more time so I'll remember, too.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>[歌唱] 再来一次，这样我也能记住。

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:31.280
<v Joey>No. I don't want to.
<v Joey>不，我不想。

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:35.760
<v Jo Carol Pierce>Joey, do you take me for a fool? I know you do.
<v Jo Carol Pierce>乔伊，你当我是傻瓜吗？我知道你是这么想的。

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:41.570
<v Joey>Oh, god, she knows I do. And I do.
<v Joey>哦，天哪，她知道我是。而且我确实是。

00:21:41.570 --> 00:22:17.870
<v Joey>[SINGING] I should have gone the way I came, like a flicker in her flame, and left her there remembering how my diamond scratched her window pane. Should've gone the way I came, in the darkness, in the rain. In the morning all that would remain is my scratch upon her window pane.
<v Joey>[歌唱] 我本该原路返回，就像她火焰中的一缕闪光，把她留在那里，回忆着我的钻石是如何划伤她的窗玻璃。我本该原路返回，在那黑暗中，在那雨中。清晨留下的只有我的划痕在她的窗玻璃上。

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:22.800
<v Joey>Jo Carol, if you keep on like this, who will every marry you?
<v Joey>乔·卡罗尔，如果你继续这样，将来谁会娶你？

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:26.590
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Suburban Girl.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，郊区女孩。

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:45.420
<v Ira Glass>Well, you're listening to This American Life, where each week we take a theme and invite a variety of writers and performers and artists of various sorts to tackle the theme. This week our theme is teenage girls. And our next story is by Rennie Sparks, a Chicago writer and musician. Her band, The Handsome Family, does the musical scoring under the story.
<v Ira Glass>欢迎收听《美国生活》，我们每周会选定一个主题，邀请各类作家、表演者和艺术家来探讨。本周的主题是“十几岁的女孩”。下一篇故事来自雷妮·斯帕克斯，一位芝加哥作家兼音乐人。她的乐队“英俊家庭”将为这则故事配乐。

00:22:45.420 --> 00:23:08.930
<v Ira Glass>This story is about a girl who is struggling in a situation where some rules-- there was a friendship, how you act with your best friend-- those rules are strict and completely binding. But all the other rules in the girl's life are totally in the air. And in the world she's in, she actually never worries about whether she's being good. Instead, she has other worries.
<v Ira Glass>这个故事讲的是一个女孩，她陷入了一种规则严苛且完全束缚人的境地——关于友谊，关于你如何对待最好的朋友。但她生活中其他所有的规则都完全是不确定的。在她所处的世界里，她从未担心过自己是否是个好女孩。相反，她有其他的烦恼。

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:27.480
<v Rennie Sparks>Dawn and me eat scrambled eggs with tall glasses of tomato juice for dinner because we're on a diet. Dawn knows how to throw up, so she eats toast and butter, too. But my fingers go so far down my throat, and still nothing comes up. I'm a fat cow.
<v Rennie Sparks>道恩和我晚餐吃炒鸡蛋，配上一大杯番茄汁，因为我们在节食。道恩知道怎么催吐，所以她还吃吐司和黄油。但我把手指伸进喉咙很深的地方，却什么也吐不出来。我真是头肥猪。

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:43.770
<v Rennie Sparks>Dawn and me are best friends. I sleep over at her house now ever since my stepfather called me a pig for eating all his cocktail onions. I'm waiting for my mom to call and beg me to come back home, but it's been almost two weeks now. I'm wondering if maybe their phone is broken.
<v Rennie Sparks>道恩是我最好的朋友。自从继父因为我吃光了他的鸡尾酒洋葱而骂我是猪之后，我就一直住在她家。我在等我妈打电话求我回家，但已经快两周了。我在想，也许她们家的电话坏了。

00:23:43.770 --> 00:24:03.640
<v Rennie Sparks>Sometimes my mom spaces on time. She drinks a lot of green Hi-C with vodka in it. I've seen her sit down with a drink in the morning just as Oprah is coming on, and then it's like next thing she knows, Hard Copy is coming on and my stepdad is walking in the door, screaming because he can't smell any dinner.
<v Rennie Sparks>有时我妈对时间很没概念。她喝很多加了伏特加的绿色Hi-C果汁。我见过她早上刚坐下喝一杯，正好赶上奥普拉的节目，接着就像是一转眼的功夫，《硬拷贝》节目就开始了，我继父走进来大喊大叫，因为他闻不到任何晚餐的味道。

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:41.800
<v Rennie Sparks>But I'm over at Dawn's house now. We share a single bed with the quilted pink covers. We stay up late smoking cigarettes, talking about love with the ashtray balanced between us on the sheets. Dawn loves guys who give long, wet kisses that make her eyes roll up in her head. I love bites that last like red splashes on my neck for weeks, so that everyone knows I've been going at it. Dawn and me both agree, though-- mostly, we just want to fall in love. Tonight we're going to the mall to fall in love.
<v Rennie Sparks>但我现在住在道恩家。我们共用一张铺着粉色绗缝被的单人床。我们熬夜抽烟，谈论爱情，烟灰缸就平衡地放在我们中间的床单上。道恩喜欢那些深吻得让她翻白眼的男人。我喜欢像红色斑点一样在脖子上持续好几周的吻痕，好让每个人都知道我一直在做那种事。不过，道恩和我达成共识——我们大体上只是想坠入爱河。今晚我们要去商场寻找爱情。

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:58.890
<v Rennie Sparks>Dawn's mom is divorced, and Dawn gets to call her Lorraine instead of Mom. We smoke Lorraine's Marlboro Lights at the kitchen table with a green ashtray. Lorraine is in the shower with her loofah pad and her pineapple-smelling shampoo, getting ready to go out with an optometrist she met at work.
